<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#stadtholder">Glossary</a> · Term · office</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stadtholder</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as stadhouder</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A stadtholder, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> stadhouder or 'stead holder', was a steward in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>, first a medieval official who stood in for an absent duke or count and later a provincial and national leader. Under the Burgundian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rulers each province had its own. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-abjuration">Act of Abjuration</a> of 1581 the office survived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> as the highest executive official of each province, appointed by its States and commander of the armed forces. Usually held by the Princes of Orange, the stadtholdership of Holland at times made its holder the de facto head of state; it became hereditary in 1747 and was abolished in 1795, when William V fled the French.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stadtholder.jpg" alt="Stadtholder" width="330" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antonis Mor · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antonio_Moro_-_Willem_I_van_Nassau.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He had clashed with <b>Stadtholder</b> Maurice of Nassau amid the conflict between Remonstrants and Counter-Remonstrants.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-13">13 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
